WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.690
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.380
So in this video, we
talk about stability

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:19.150
and, the opposite of stability,
the decays of nuclei.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:20.830
OK, so let's look
at this diagram

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:24.100
first, which shows the
number of protons--

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.130
the number of protons and
the number of neutrons here.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:30.670
And you find that
the stable elements

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.640
sit in this so-called
Valley of Stability.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.950
So there's a certain part of
our nuclei which are stable.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.920
This valley follows directly
out of the discussion

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.550
of the binding energy, where
you can calculate the optimal Z

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:50.570
value such that the
mass is minimal.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:55.540
It's about A half in this
area here at low masses,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:02.190
and about 40% of A the higher
part-- at high value of mass.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.820
We already discussed that the
binding energy has this form

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.530
here, with a maximum
around the mass of iron,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.130
which is like somewhere here--

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.920
50 some.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.440
OK, so it's
energetically favorable

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.270
for some unstable
nuclei to break apart

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.490
in so-called fission processes.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.800
And it's also
energetically favorable

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:36.390
to fuse together
to create energy.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.810
And we'll talk about the
applications of those processes

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.045
later on.

00:01:41.045 --> 00:01:42.420
But for now, we
want to just look

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.180
at a radioactive nuclide, which
can decay in various ways.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.260
And so the first, most prominent
decays we want to discuss

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.080
here-- and then we'll
follow up a little later--

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.856
is an alpha decay,
which is splitting up--

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:07.590
a mother particle splits up
into daughter particle nuclei

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.130
and helium-- which is
an alpha particle--

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:15.780
or the emission of electrons,
positrons, or the capture of

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.680
electrons as well.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.890
It is also possible,
but rather rare,

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:26.270
that a nuclei just spits out a
proton, or spits out a neutron.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.910
So this is possible,
but it's rather rare.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.400
All right, so let's look
at the alpha decay first.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.080
As I said, we start from a
rather large, heavy nuclei.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.070
And it seems possible
for it to spit out

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:45.570
helium or an alpha particle.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.350
So the first thing we want to--
the view we want to have here

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:53.490
is of the potential in which
the alpha particle sits.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.420
So the alpha particle
sees a really deep well

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.040
of the nuclear potential.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.970
And it also sees this
boundary here, this barrier,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.136
of the Coulomb potential.

00:03:05.136 --> 00:03:08.040
So for the alpha
particle to be emitted,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.880
it needs to break through
this Coulomb potential here.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.550
And you can calculate the
likelihood using quantum

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.850
mechanics-- the quantum
tunneling likelihood--

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.610
in order to figure
out how stable

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.560
an individual particle is.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.290
Here, I just wanted
to show you something.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:29.560
This plot here shows you the
lifetime of an unstable nuclei,

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.580
for various sorts, as a
function of the energy.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.260
And what you see here is it's
very strong energy dependent.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.440
This is a logarithmic plot.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.410
And what you see is
the lifetime seems

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:44.280
to be rather, rather short
when the emitted particle has

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.510
a lot of energy.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.140
And that can be explained by
this plot here very easily.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.660
In order for this particle--
the energy of this particle,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.710
when it goes here, is dependent
on where this particle sits

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.370
in this potential, right?

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:01.410
So particles which sit
at very high values here

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:06.570
will have a high likelihood to
tunnel through this barrier,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.520
and therefore a short lifetime.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.790
Hence, this particle
has a lot of energy

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.550
after it's been emitted.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:19.380
So we see lifetimes in the
range of 10 nanoseconds to 10

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.519
to the 17 years
for some examples.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:23.580
So there's this huge
variation depending

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.850
on where the alpha particle
sits in this potential.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.250
We can have a discussion
of the energetics involved.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:36.240
And we use our very same
formula for the binding energy.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:42.140
So you write this down
here, including your helium

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:46.940
here, and then just
figuring out what

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.560
are the contribution of
the individual terms.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.590
And since we observe
experimentally

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.470
alpha decays only for heavy
particles, in this discussion

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:59.510
here, we can assume that
Z is approximately 0.041

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:01.070
times the mass number.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.820
And so the energy of the
emitted alpha particle for this

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:09.290
to be able to occur
has to be positive.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:14.120
And so we find this to be
possible for A values starting

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.890
from about 150.

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.140
Experimentally, you
observe this to start

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:22.920
happening at about 200.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.570
All right, the next thought we
want to discuss is beta decay.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.560
So this is shown in this
diagram here, where--

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.290
starting from carbon-14
decays, for example--

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:36.210
carbon-14, you will see
this in the next recitation,

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.500
is very useful probe in
order to date living things,

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.697
and date when they were
not living anymore.

00:05:42.697 --> 00:05:47.460
And you will discuss why and
how you can actually do this.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.520
But for now, carbon-14
can decay via beta decay.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.360
And what you find is
nitrogen, an anti-electron,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.770
and an electron.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.040
This is a beta decay,
or beta minus decay.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.720
And using our particle
physics discussion,

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.670
we can easily understand this.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:09.860
The neutron is transformed
into a proton via electroweak

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.680
processes with a W.
The electron comes out,

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.990
the anti-neutrino comes out.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:17.600
And similarly, we can
look at carbon-10 here.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.690
Into boron is a
neutrino and a positron.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:26.440
And again here, we have
a W plus in the decay.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.450
So now, we can, again,
use our binding energy

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:29.805
in order to understand this.

00:06:29.805 --> 00:06:39.180
So what we want to do here,
for constant mass numbers,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.990
you want to plot the binding
energy for the individual atoms

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:45.610
or nuclei.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:50.920
And so if you do this for odd
A, those as where the pairing

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:56.650
term doesn't contribute, you
find this nice quadratic term.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:02.770
And you can find beta decays in
each of those instances here.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:06.340
For A even, you have the
question whether or not

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.940
Z is odd or N is odd--

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:13.900
oh sorry, Z and N are
odd, or Z and N are even.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:17.860
So you have two
quadratic functions here.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:21.620
And you find the beta decay
between one and the other.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:26.440
And so that's interesting
you find those decays chains,

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:28.510
based on where you
start in the chain,

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.880
you have the possibility
to go back and forth.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.750
And because of the
even and odd pattern,

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:39.700
the lifetime of the decay
can vary quite tremendously

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:44.200
between those individual states.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.060
Last but not least,
election capture--

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:49.810
if you have a very
massive nuclei and atom,

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.400
and the electrons-- you know,
thinking about a cloud around

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:52.900
this--

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:55.210
some of the electrons can
come very, very close,

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.770
and be captured into the nuclei.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.270
And you find this here.

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:05.620
The time direction
goes up, proton

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.300
captures via the
weak interaction.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:11.140
The electron becomes a
neutron and emits a neutrino.

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:17.670
And an example is the electron
capture of krypton into boron.

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:22.440
All right, so we start to get
some sort of understanding

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.900
of nuclear decays.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.660
We find this Valley
of Stability here.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:33.270
We find, in a large
range for lower numbers

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:36.510
of Z, beta decays.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:40.260
For higher numbers of Z,
we find beta plus decays.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.830
And for very heavy nuclei,
we find alpha decays.

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:51.650
Proton decays, seen at
the very boundary here,

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:56.360
and nuclear fission processes
where the nuclei spontaneously

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:59.090
breaks up, we haven't
discussed in detail.

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.240
But you can think about
them very similarly

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.580
to the alpha decay.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.280
As you already kind
of probably saw

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.320
from the discussion
of the beta decay,

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.950
it's possible to have
rather long decay chains.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:15.720
So you start from
radioactive nuclei,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.300
which then decays, and
decays, and decays, and decays

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.140
in those kind of chains.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:23.700
So this is two examples
here-- the thorium chain

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.590
and the uranium chain--

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:29.490
creating all kinds of
other new elements.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:32.040
And on the uranium chain,
it's very interesting

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:35.790
to say uranium is
part of our core.

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.900
If you build a house
and you build--

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:43.050
if you build-- the
foundation is concrete,

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.540
you probably have
some uranium in there,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.990
which then, in this decay
chain, generates radium.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.390
And therefore, if you build
a house with concrete,

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.180
you want to have a measure to
get rid of the radium which

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.230
is just floating around.

00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.880
All right, so this
is it for now.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.220
We continue the discussion with
more detailed understanding,

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.830
or detailed discussion,
on how those decay

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:10.480
processes are possible, and
what we can learn from them.

